<article class="env-proposition" id="ED0101">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/ED0101">Proposition <span data-tag="ED0101">1.2.12.4</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category containing an object $Y$. The object $Y$ is strongly final if and only if, for every object $X \in \calC $, the Kan complex $\Hom ^{\rght }_{\calC }(X,Y)$ is contractible. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The “only if” direction is clear: the space $\Hom ^{\rght }_{\calC }(X,Y)$ is the fiber of the projection $p: \calC _{/Y} \rightarrow \calC $ over the vertex $X$. If $p$ is a trivial fibration, then the fiber is a contractible Kan complex. Since $p$ is a right fibration (Proposition <a href="/tag/EEE6AE" data-tag="EEE6AE">2.1.2.1</a>), the converse holds as well (Lemma <a href="/tag/0628D7" data-tag="0628D7">2.1.3.4</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>